<!--
    Fragment: backend-auth  (BROKERED variant)

    Uploaded as fragment id "map-backend-auth" when identity_mode =
    "brokered". This is the default for both profiles.

    THIS FRAGMENT DELIBERATELY REPLACES THE CALLER'S TOKEN. Read why before
    changing it.

    In brokered mode the human holds NO RBAC on the Foundry resource. Only
    the gateway's managed identity does. The bypass is therefore eliminated
    by CAPABILITY rather than by network topology: a developer cannot call
    Foundry directly from anywhere - not from the corporate network, not over
    VPN or ExpressRoute, not by resolving the hostname manually - because
    they have no permission, and no network position confers one.

    That is strictly stronger than an NSG rule, which is only as good as the
    last person who edited it.

    WHAT THIS COSTS, stated plainly:

      * Foundry no longer authenticates the human. It authenticates the
        gateway. The second, independent authorization check that
        passthrough mode gets for free is gone.
      * Foundry resource logs attribute every call to one service principal.
        Human attribution lives in gateway telemetry and in the
        user_security_context below.
      * Conditional Access and token lifetime no longer apply at the model
        boundary; they apply only at the gateway boundary.
      * APIM becomes a CONFUSED DEPUTY: a permanently privileged caller that
        will invoke the model for anyone its policy admits. A policy
        misconfiguration is no longer caught downstream - it is the whole
        security boundary.

    The human identity is still carried to the model, but as CONTEXT rather
    than as the authenticating principal. user_security_context is the
    first-party mechanism for exactly this: Microsoft documents it for
    "application-mediated architecture ... or AI gateway" scenarios, and
    recommends passing EndUserId and SourceIP so that SOC analysts can
    investigate AI alerts involving a gateway.

    See docs/identity-modes.md.
-->
<fragment>
    <set-variable name="identity-mode" value="brokered" />

    <!--
        Replace the caller's Authorization with a token for the gateway's
        own managed identity.

        The resource is a named value rather than a literal because the
        accepted audience must match what Foundry actually issues tokens
        for - empirically "https://ai.azure.com", NOT the "/.default" scope
        string used to request it. See gate G1 in
        docs/platform-validation.md.
    -->
    <authentication-managed-identity resource="{{map-backend-mi-resource}}" />

    <!--
        Carry the validated human identity as security context.

        This runs AFTER schema validation on purpose. The request allowlist
        sets additionalProperties:false, so injecting this field before
        validation would make the gateway reject its own addition.

        end_user_id is the validated Entra object ID - a protected
        pseudonymous identifier, never a UPN, email, or display name.
        Microsoft's guidance is explicit that sensitive personal information
        must not be placed in this field.
    -->
    <set-variable name="broker-body" value="@(context.Request.Body.As&lt;JObject&gt;(preserveContent: true))" />

    <set-body>@{
        var body = (JObject)context.Variables["broker-body"];

        var securityContext = new JObject();
        securityContext["end_user_id"] = context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;string&gt;("claim-oid", "");
        securityContext["source_ip"] = context.Request.IpAddress;
        securityContext["application_name"] = "mission-apimpossible";

        body["user_security_context"] = securityContext;
        return body.ToString(Newtonsoft.Json.Formatting.None);
    }</set-body>
</fragment>
